  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...High pressure builds in through mid week.\n\nLate this morning, regional satellite and radar imagery showed\nan area of enhanced/textured cloudiness and isolated showers \ncoming off the Blue Ridge into some the valleys of western and \ncentral VA. This appears to be tied to a weak mid-level impulse \nin the westerly flow aloft. Overall, the depth of moisture has \ndiminished in the wake of last night's cold frontal passage, so \nas the aforementioned short-wave traverses the region into early\nafternoon, its main impact should be restricted to \nisolated/widely scattered showers and garden variety \nthunderstorms for portions of VA and far southern MD. Outside of\nthat, we're looking at rain-free conditions for the balance of\nthe day, with seasonable temperatures ranging from the 70s over\nthe higher terrain of the Alleghenies, to the mid-upper 80s\nacross the metroplex. \n\nSurface high pressure will be the primary controlling factor, as\nit builds across the Mid-Atlantic into Wednesday, with dry \nconditions expected. A cold front will approach from the Great \nLakes, bringing an increasing chance of showers and perhaps a \nthunderstorm by late Wednesday night.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Daily chances for showers and thunderstorms \nreturn Thursday, then continue Friday through Sunday.\n\nAlthough timing remains a bit uncertain, a cold front looks to\nenter the area Thursday morning, then slowly cross through\nThursday night. Questions regarding early day precip and cloud\ncover lend to uncertainty in how much instability builds\nThursday afternoon, though a few stronger storms are certainly\npossible given increased flow aloft and lift along the front.\n\nModel guidance is in relatively good agreement that the longwave \npattern across the CONUS will amplify significantly heading into \nnext weekend as unseasonably deep troughing digs across the Great \nLakes and Ohio Valley. While there is some spread with respect to \njust how deep this trough will be and its exact east/west placement, \nnearly all solutions place us downstream of the trough, which will \nkeep us in southwesterly flow aloft and keep the pattern active. \nDaily chances for showers and thunderstorms are expected within the \nzone of large scale ascent downstream of the longwave trough \nsharpening to our west. Depending on how much instability develops, \nsome of these storms could potentially be on the stronger side. \nTemperatures are forecast to run slightly below normal, with highs \nin the upper 70s and low 80s, and overnight lows in the 60s.\n\nMost solutions have the longwave trough making some eastward \nprogress by Sunday night into Monday, which should result in a cold \nfront passing through. Continued below normal temperatures and drier \nconditions are forecast behind the front for Monday.",
